|~ qlang operand catalog — control family. Each operand BindStep
   below sits directly under the per-site error tags it raises;
   the operand's `:throws` Vec references those tags by name. ~|

:if
  |~~ Three-branch conditional: `if cond ~(then) ~(else)`. The
      condition is a value computed at the call, each branch a quote
      applied to pipeValue, and only the selected branch runs.

      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "positive")
      ~(-1 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "non-positive")
      ~({:age 25} | if (/age | gte 18) ~("adult") ~("minor") | eq "adult")

      Truthiness: `null` and `false` are falsy; every other value —
      including `0`, `""`, `[]`, `{}`, `#[]` — is truthy. ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/if
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:any :pipeline :pipeline]
   }

::IfThenNotQuoteError
  |~~ The then branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | if true "yes" ~("no")
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfThenNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::IfElseNotQuoteError
  |~~ The else branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | if true ~("yes") "no"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfElseNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:when
  |~~ Guarded transform: `when cond ~(then)`. When cond is truthy,
      the then sub-pipeline fires against pipeValue; otherwise
      pipeValue passes through unchanged. Use for "do X only if
      condition holds, else leave the value alone".

      ~(5 | when (gt 0) ~(add 100) | eq 105)
      ~(-5 | when (gt 0) ~(add 100) | eq -5)
      ~({:level "warn"} | when (/level | eq "error") ~("ALERT") | eq {:level "warn"})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/when
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:any :pipeline]
   }

::WhenBranchNotQuoteError
  |~~ The branch of `when` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `when (gt 0) ~(add 100)`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | when true "yes"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::WhenBranchNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:unless
  |~~ Inverse of `when`: `unless cond ~(then)` fires the then
      sub-pipeline only when cond is falsy; truthy pipeValue passes
      through unchanged. Reads naturally for fallback / default
      patterns ("if no real value, supply this one").

      ~(null | unless (type | eq ::number) ~(0) | eq 0)
      ~(42 | unless (type | eq ::number) ~(0) | eq 42)
      ~({:name null} | unless /name ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/unless
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:any :pipeline]
   }

::UnlessBranchNotQuoteError
  |~~ The branch of `unless` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `unless (gt 0) ~(0)`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | unless false "yes"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::UnlessBranchNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:coalesce
  |~~ Walks the captured alternatives in order, evaluating each
      against pipeValue, and returns the first result that is
      neither null nor an error value. Every other value —
      including the falsy ones false, 0, "", [], {}, #[] — short-
      circuits the iteration. All-skip chain yields null. Use the
      `coalesce ~(/key) ~(fallback)` shape to read "use /key when
      defined, else fallback" — `/key` produces an error on a
      missing-key Map, which the skip rule treats the same as
      null.

      ~({:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "alice")
      ~({:nickname "ace" :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "ace")
      ~({:a 2 :b 3} | coalesce ~(/missing) ~("fallback") | eq "fallback")
      ~({:nickname null :name null} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/coalesce
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:pipeline]}

::CoalesceNoAlternativesError
  |~~ `coalesce` requires at least one captured alternative sub-pipeline.
      ~(
        {} | coalesce
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::CoalesceNoAlternativesError]
       )
  ~~|

::CoalesceAlternativeNotQuoteError
  |~~ Every alternative of `coalesce` is code it runs only when reached, so
      each takes a quote, `coalesce ~(/b) ~(/a)`; `:index` counts the modifiers from 1.
      ~(
        {:a 1} | coalesce /a
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::CoalesceAlternativeNotQuoteError 1 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:firstTruthy
  |~~ Walks the captured alternatives in order, evaluating each
      against pipeValue, and returns the first truthy result.
      Skip rule covers null, false, and error values. All-skip
      chain yields null. Reach for `firstTruthy` when false is a
      sentinel meaning "no value"; reach for `coalesce` when
      false is a valid explicit setting.

      ~({:override false :default "fallback"} | firstTruthy ~(/override) ~(/default) | eq "fallback")
      ~({:override "win" :default "fallback"} | firstTruthy ~(/override) ~(/default) | eq "win")
      ~({:a 2} | firstTruthy ~(/missing) ~(/a) | eq 2)
      ~(5 | firstTruthy ~(eq 10) ~(eq 5) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/firstTruthy
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:pipeline]}

::FirstTruthyNoAlternativesError
  |~~ `firstTruthy` requires at least one captured alternative sub-pipeline.
      ~(
        {} | firstTruthy
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::FirstTruthyNoAlternativesError]
       )
  ~~|

::FirstTruthyAlternativeNotQuoteError
  |~~ Every alternative of `firstTruthy` is code it runs only when reached, so
      each takes a quote, `firstTruthy ~(/b) ~(/a)`; `:index` counts the modifiers from 1.
      ~(
        {:a 1} | firstTruthy /a
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::FirstTruthyAlternativeNotQuoteError 1 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:cond
  |~~ Multi-way dispatch over alternating (predicate, branch)
      sub-pipeline pairs. Walks captured args in pairs; returns
      the result of the first branch whose predicate evaluates
      truthy against pipeValue. If captured-arg count is odd, the
      trailing arg is the default branch. If even and no match,
      returns null. Replaces nested-if chains with a flat
      catalog.

      ~(85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "B")
      ~(65 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "F")
      ~({:role "admin"} | cond ~(/role | eq "admin") ~("full") ~(/role | eq "user") ~("limited") | eq "full")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/cond
            :category :control
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:pipeline]}

::CondNoBranchesError
  |~~ `cond` requires at least one (predicate, branch) pair plus an optional trailing default.
      ~(
        5 | cond
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::CondNoBranchesError]
       )
  ~~|

::CondClauseNotQuoteError
  |~~ Every clause of `cond` is code it runs only when reached, so
      each takes a quote, `cond ~(gt 0) ~("positive") ~("other")`; `:index` counts the modifiers from 1.
      ~(
        5 | cond true ~("yes")
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::CondClauseNotQuoteError 1 ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|
